forbes released picks for its 2022  fintech 50 list which highlights 50 of  the most innovative companies in the  financial technology space  to qualify for the fintech 50 a company  must either have headquarters or  substantial operations in the us and be  private as of the date of publication  the startups on the fintech 50 cover a  wide range of financial services and  cater to both regular consumers as well  as b2b  what we saw this year was that it was  our hardest year yet in trying to  determine what are the 50 most  innovative fintech companies there's a  ton of energy flowing into the space a  ton of money  fintech startups received about 130  billion in funding in 2021 compared with  49 billion in 2020 another thing that  resulted from from all of this energy  and money flowing into the space that  this year we had 25 newcomers to the  west and so half of the list were  companies that have never been on the  list before we've never had that many  newcomers  here is an example of five of this  year's 50 list makers and the specific  areas they cover ftx is probably one of  the most well-known on the list  especially for investors in  cryptocurrencies  based in nassau capital of the bahamas  it's one of the largest crypto trading  exchanges in the world handling about 11  of the 2.4 trillion dollars in  derivatives traded each month a 500  million dollar fundraise this past  january took the company's valuation to  32 billion dollars its separate us  entity is valued at eight billion  dollars and ftx has been spending  hundreds of millions of dollars on  marketing signing up celebrity brand  ambassadors such as tom brady david  ortiz and kevin o'leary the company's  co-founders are ceo sam bankman freed  who is the world's second richest crypto  billionaire and cto gary wang  chainalysis has been involved with  helping to solve some of the world's  highest profile criminal cases involving  digital assets including the seizure of  3.5 billion dollars worth of crypto by  the irs criminal investigation unit the  new york based company now screens one  trillion dollars in transaction value  each month across all cryptocurrency  assets and itself is valued at 8.6  billion dollars  over the past year chanalysis has  increased its customer base by 75  and now has more than 750 clients in 70  countries its co-founders are ceo  michael groninger a phd in quantum  mechanics who previously co-founded the  crypto exchange kraken and chief  strategy officer jonathan levin novo is  aiming to replace the big banks as the  go-to provider of checking accounts and  other banking services for small  businesses  based in miami the company took a slow  and steady approach it was founded in  2016 but did not launch until two years  later it got by on under seven million  dollars in seed capital and a federal  ppp forgivable loan until mid 2021 when  it started raising serious expansion  funds novo's latest valuation is 700  million dollars and its customer base  grew exponentially from 24 000 to 140  000 in 2021 capitalizing on the burst of  small business creation in the us its  co-founders are ceo michael rangel and  cto tyler mcintyre plaid is a san  francisco-based company which helps  fintech apps such as venmo and coinbase  connect to customer bank accounts  allowing for deposits and payments  its latest valuation is 13.4 billion  dollars and it paid 250 million dollars  to acquire cognito an identity  verification service and know your  customer or kyc compliance provider  plaid has raised 735 million dollars in  venture capital from altimeter capital  anderson horowitz and others its  co-founders are ceo zach parrott and  former cto william hockey who was the  co-founder of column a newcomer on this  year's fintech 50. finally bridget is a  financial app aimed at helping americans  living paycheck to paycheck build up  their money management skills and credit  scores while avoiding bank overdrafts  and payday advance loans the  subscription-based service links a  user's bank account and approves them  for an interest-free loan of 50 to 250  a free version of the app provides  advice but no loans  the company is valued at 140 million  dollars and has almost a half a million  paying users and three million accounts  using the free features the new york  based company was co-founded by ceo  zubin matthews who was an investment  maker with deutsche bank for a decade  and cto hamel kathari who was a member  of the 2021 forbes 30 under 30 list
 

